The root cause: `setInterval` measures elapsed milliseconds, not wall-clock time. When DST shifts happen, 86,400,000 ms no longer equals one calendar day. The job drifts because it keeps running at the same elapsed-time offset, not the same wall-clock hour.

**Fix:** Schedule to the next intended time (e.g., midnight), then reschedule after each run:

```javascript
function scheduleNightlyJob(run) {
  function schedule() {
    const now = new Date();
    const tomorrow = new Date(now);
    tomorrow.setDate(tomorrow.getDate() + 1);
    tomorrow.setHours(0, 0, 0, 0); // next midnight

    const msUntilNext = tomorrow - now;
    setTimeout(() => {
      run();
      schedule(); // reschedule after run
    }, msUntilNext);
  }
  schedule();
}
```

